=== lesson
id: py-comp-set
title: Set comprehensions
summary: A set comprehension like `{len(w) for w in words}` collects only the different results. You'll also meet generator expressions, which hand out values one at a time without building a list, and use them with `sum()`, `max()`, `min()`, `any()` and `all()`.

=== learn
### A set in one line
Curly braces with a single expression at the front, and no colon, make a **set comprehension**. It works like a list comprehension, but the result is a set, so duplicates disappear:
```python
nums = [1, 4, 7, 2, 5]
remainders = {n % 3 for n in nums}
print(remainders)
print(len(remainders))
```
```output
{1, 2}
2
```
The remainders are 1, 1, 1, 2 and 2, but a set keeps each value once. The matching loop starts from an empty set and uses `add`:
```python
remainders = set()
for n in nums:
    remainders.add(n % 3)
```
As in the sets lesson, a set has no order. Print `sorted(...)` of a set of strings, so that the output is the same every time.

### Only the different results
A set comprehension answers "which different values come out?". Compare it with a list comprehension, which keeps every result:
```python
words = ["tea", "jam", "toast", "egg", "bread"]
print([len(w) for w in words])
print(sorted({len(w) for w in words}))
print(len({len(w) for w in words}), "different lengths")
```
```output
[3, 3, 5, 3, 5]
[3, 5]
2 different lengths
```
The list has one length per word. The set has each length once, so `len()` of it counts how many **different** lengths there are.

### Keeping only some items
An `if` at the end filters, exactly as in the other comprehensions. The test checks the original item, before the expression is worked out:
```python
words = ["Apple", "fig", "apple", "Kiwi", "kiwi", "plum"]
long_ones = {w.lower() for w in words if len(w) > 3}
print(sorted(long_ones))
```
```output
['apple', 'kiwi', 'plum']
```
"fig" is skipped by the filter. "Apple" and "apple" both become "apple", and "Kiwi" and "kiwi" both become "kiwi", so the set holds 3 words.

### A comprehension or set([...])?
You could also build the list first and then turn it into a set:
```python
nums = [3, 1, 3, 2, 1]
a = set([n * 10 for n in nums])
b = {n * 10 for n in nums}
print(a == b)
print(sorted(b))
```
```output
True
[10, 20, 30]
```
Both give the same set, but `set([...])` first builds a whole list, only to throw it away. The set comprehension adds each result straight into the set.

When you don't need to change the items at all, you don't need a comprehension either: `set(nums)` is enough.

> An empty pair of braces is a dictionary. A set comprehension with nothing to loop over gives an empty **set**, which prints as `set()`: `{n for n in []}` is `set()`.

### Generator expressions
Round brackets around a comprehension don't make a "tuple comprehension". They make a **generator expression**: something that hands out the values **one at a time**, only when asked, without storing them all in a list.
```python
squares = (n * n for n in range(1, 4))
print(type(squares))
print(list(squares))
print(list(squares))
```
```output
<class 'generator'>
[1, 4, 9]
[]
```
`list(...)` asks for every value, so the first `list` gets 1, 4 and 9. A generator can only be used **once**: after it has handed out its last value it is empty, so the second `list` gets nothing.

Printing a generator itself only shows something like `<generator object <genexpr> at 0x7f...>`, not its values.

### sum() with a generator expression
`sum(...)` adds up all the numbers it is given: from a list, a range, or a generator expression. When a generator expression is the **only** thing inside the call's brackets, it needs no brackets of its own:
```python
prices = [3, 5, 8]
print(sum(prices))
print(sum(p * 2 for p in prices))
print(sum(p for p in prices if p > 4))
print(sum(1 for p in prices if p > 4))
```
```output
16
32
13
2
```
`sum(p * 2 for p in prices)` adds each doubled price as it comes, without building a list of doubled prices first. The last line adds 1 for every price over 4, so it **counts** them. With nothing to add, `sum` gives 0.

With anything else inside the brackets, the generator needs its own brackets: `sum(p for p in prices, 10)` is `SyntaxError: Generator expression must be parenthesized`.

### max() and min() with a generator expression
You've used `max(3, 7)` with separate numbers. Given one collection, `max(...)` and `min(...)` find its biggest and smallest item:
```python
words = ["fig", "banana", "kiwi"]
print(max(len(w) for w in words))
print(min(len(w) for w in words))
print(max([4, 9, 2]))
```
```output
6
3
9
```
`max(len(w) for w in words)` finds the longest length without building a list of lengths. If there is nothing to compare, they stop with an error:
```python
print(max(n for n in [5, 8] if n > 10))
```
```output
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 1, in <module>
    print(max(n for n in [5, 8] if n > 10))
          ~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
ValueError: max() iterable argument is empty
```
No number passes the filter, so `max` gets nothing at all.

### any() and all()
`any(...)` answers "is **at least one** of them True?", and `all(...)` answers "are **all** of them True?":
```python
temps = [18, 25, 31]
print(any(t > 30 for t in temps))
print(all(t > 20 for t in temps))
print(all(t > 10 for t in temps))
```
```output
True
False
True
```
31 is over 30, so `any` is True. 18 isn't over 20, so `all(t > 20 ...)` is False. With nothing to check at all, `any` gives False (nothing was True) and `all` gives True (nothing failed).

Both stop as soon as they know the answer: `any` stops at the first True, and `all` at the first False. Because a generator only works out values when asked, the rest are never worked out at all:
```python
nums = [4, 0, 2]
print(any(10 / n > 2 for n in nums))
```
```output
True
```
For 4, `10 / 4` is 2.5, which is more than 2, so `any` stops right there and `10 / 0` never runs. A list comprehension inside `any([...])` would work out every item first, and crash with ZeroDivisionError.

### Which brackets?

| You write | You get | Good for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `[x * 2 for x in nums]` | a list: every result, in order | keeping the results to use them again |
| `{x * 2 for x in nums}` | a set: each different result once | unique values, quick `in` checks |
| `{x: x * 2 for x in nums}` | a dictionary | looking values up by key |
| `sum(x * 2 for x in nums)` | a generator, used straight away | feeding `sum`, `max`, `min`, `any`, `all` |

A generator expression is the right choice when the values are only needed **once**, by the function around it. When you need the results again later, or want to print them, build a list or a set instead.

=== keypoints
- `{expr for x in items}` builds a set: each different result once. It does the job of `s = set()` and `s.add(expr)` in a loop. `len()` of it counts the different results.
- An `if` at the end filters the items before the expression is worked out.
- `{...}` with a colon is a dictionary; without one it's a set. `{n for n in []}` is an empty set, `set()`.
- `set([...])` builds a list first and then a set; a set comprehension builds the set directly. `set(items)` is enough when nothing changes.
- `(expr for x in items)` is a generator expression: it hands out values one at a time, only when asked, and can be used only once.
- Inside `sum()`, `max()`, `min()`, `any()` and `all()` a generator needs no extra brackets when it's the only argument: `sum(p * 2 for p in prices)`. `sum(1 for x in items if test)` counts matches.
- `any` is True if at least one value is True, `all` if every value is. Both stop early. `max`/`min` of nothing is a ValueError.

=== example: What's in the basket?
intro: Set comprehensions find the different values, and generator expressions feed sum, max and any.
```python
basket = ["apple", "Pear", "apple", "fig", "pear", "kiwi"]
kinds = {f.lower() for f in basket}
print(sorted(kinds))
print(len(basket), "items,", len(kinds), "kinds")
long_kinds = {f.lower() for f in basket if len(f) > 3}
print(sorted(long_kinds))
print(sum(len(f) for f in basket))
print(max(len(f) for f in basket))
print(any(f.startswith("k") for f in basket))
print(all(len(f) > 2 for f in basket))
```
1: Six items, but some are the same fruit with different capital letters.
2: Lowercasing first makes "Pear" and "pear" the same, and the set keeps each kind once.
3: A set of strings is printed with sorted(), so the order is always the same.
4: 6 items, but only 4 different kinds.
5: The filter skips "fig" before it is lowercased.
7: A generator expression inside sum: the lengths are added one by one, and no list of lengths is built.
8: max finds the longest name: "apple" with 5 letters.
9: any stops as soon as one fruit starts with "k": kiwi does.
10: all checks that every name is longer than 2 letters: True.

=== example: Checking a password
intro: any() and all() with generator expressions turn a list of rules into one line each.
```python
password = "Sunny2day"
has_digit = any(ch.isdigit() for ch in password)
has_upper = any("A" <= ch <= "Z" for ch in password)
long_enough = len(password) >= 8
print(has_digit, has_upper, long_enough)
print(all([has_digit, has_upper, long_enough]))
digits = sum(1 for ch in password if ch.isdigit())
print(digits, "digit(s)")
print(sorted({ch.lower() for ch in password if not ch.isdigit()}))
```
1: A password to check.
2: any() goes through the characters and stops at the first digit, the 2.
3: A capital letter is between "A" and "Z". The S at the start passes, so any() stops at once.
4: An ordinary comparison: 9 characters is at least 8.
5: All three rules pass.
6: all() of a list of three booleans: True, because every one is True.
7: Adding 1 for every digit counts them.
9: A set comprehension: the different letters (every character that is not a digit), lowercased and printed in order.

=== animation: The set ignores repeats
view: set lengths
vars w
intro: Each word adds its length to the set, but a length that's already there doesn't go in twice. Then the comprehension builds the same set in one line.
```python
words = ["tea", "jam", "toast", "egg", "bread"]
lengths = set()
for w in words:
    lengths.add(len(w))
print(sorted(lengths))
quick = {len(w) for w in words}
print(sorted(quick))
```
1: Five words. Their lengths are 3, 3, 5, 3 and 5.
2: An empty set. Remember: {} would be an empty dictionary.
3: Take the next word. When there are none left, the loop ends.
4: len({w!r}) is {len(w)}. {'It is already in the set, so nothing changes.' if len(w) in lengths else 'It is new, so it goes into the set.'}
5: Only 2 different lengths, although there were 5 words.
6: The set comprehension does the same in one line: each length once.
7: The same set: {sorted(quick)}.

=== animation: any() stops early
view: vars
intro: Each time any() asks for the next value, the generator works out one more item. As soon as a value is True, any() stops, so the rest of the list is never checked.
```python
def is_big(n):
    print("checking", n)
    return n > 10

nums = [4, 15, 8, 30]
found = any(is_big(n) for n in nums)
print("found:", found)
```
1: is_big prints which number it checks, so you can see how many get checked.
2: Checking {n}.
3: {n} > 10 is {n > 10}.
5: Four numbers. 15 and 30 are big.
6: any() asks the generator for values one at a time. Watch the output: it stops after 15.
7: Only 4 and 15 were checked. 8 and 30 were never looked at, because any() already knew the answer.

=== task: predict easy
title: Odd or even, once each
code:
```python
parity = {n % 2 for n in [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]}
print(sorted(parity))
print(len(parity))
```
wrong[[1, 0, 1, 0, 1]\n5]: Curly braces without a colon make a set, and a set keeps each value once. Five numbers give only the two remainders 0 and 1.
wrong[[0, 1]\n5]: `len` counts the items in the set, and the set holds only 0 and 1: 2 items.
wrong[[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\n5]: The expression is `n % 2`, the remainder after dividing by 2, not `n` itself.
hint: Work out `n % 2` for each number: 1, 0, 1, 0, 1.
hint: What does a set do with repeated values?
right: The remainders are 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, and a set keeps each value only once: 0 and 1. `sorted` prints them as `[0, 1]`, and the set has 2 items.
wrong: `n % 2` gives 1, 0, 1, 0, 1. The set comprehension keeps the different values, 0 and 1, so it prints `[0, 1]` and has length 2.

=== task: mcq easy
title: The different lengths
prompt: `words` is a list of words. Which expression gives a **set** of the different word lengths?
* `{len(w) for w in words}` :: Right. Curly braces with no colon make a set comprehension, so each length appears once.
- `[len(w) for w in words]` :: Square brackets make a list, which keeps every length, repeats included.
- `{w: len(w) for w in words}` :: The colon makes this a dictionary, mapping each word to its length.
- `(len(w) for w in words)` :: Round brackets make a generator expression, which hands out the lengths one at a time. It isn't a set.
hint: Which brackets make a set?
hint: A colon inside curly braces makes a dictionary instead.
right: `{len(w) for w in words}` collects the lengths in a set, so repeated lengths are kept once.
wrong: A set comprehension uses curly braces with a single expression and no colon: `{len(w) for w in words}`.

=== task: fill easy
title: First letters
prompt: Fill in the blank so that the program prints the different first letters:

```output
['a', 'b']
```
code:
```python
words = ["apple", "bread", "avocado", "bean"]
firsts = {?? for w in words}
print(sorted(firsts))
```
answer: w[0]
wrong[w]: That collects the whole words: `['apple', 'avocado', 'bean', 'bread']`. The first letter of `w` is `w[0]`.
wrong[w[1]]: Index 1 is the **second** letter. The first letter is at index 0.
hint: The expression is worked out for each word. What gives a word's first letter?
hint: Indexes start at 0.
right: `w[0]` gives each word's first letter: a, b, a, b. The set keeps each one once, and `sorted` prints them in order.
wrong: Put `w[0]` at the front: the first letter of each word. The set drops the repeats, leaving a and b.

=== task: code easy
title: The different squares
prompt: `nums` is a list of whole numbers that is already set, and the checks try several lists. Use a set comprehension to create `squares`: the set of the different squares of the numbers. For `nums = [1, -1, 2]`, `squares` is `{1, 4}`.
starter:
```python
# nums is already set before your code runs
squares = set()
```
solution:
```python
squares = {n * n for n in nums}
```
tests:
- squares
cases:
- nums = [1, -1, 2]
- nums = []
- nums = [3]
- nums = [2, -2, 2]
fail[1]: 1 and -1 both square to 1, and 2 squares to 4, so the set is `{1, 4}`. Use a set comprehension: `{n * n for n in nums}`.
fail[3]: 3 squared is 9: `{9}`.
error[NameError]: Inside the braces, use the same name as in the `for` part.
forbid[.add(]: Build the set with a comprehension instead of `.add()` in a loop.
hint: The shape is `{expression for n in nums}`.
hint: The expression is the square of `n`.
hint: `squares = {n * n for n in nums}`
right: The set comprehension squares each number and keeps each result once, so 1 and -1 give a single 1. An empty list gives an empty set.
wrong: `squares = {n * n for n in nums}`: curly braces, no colon, the square at the front.

=== task: predict easy
title: Used up
code:
```python
g = (c.upper() for c in "ab")
print(list(g))
print(list(g))
```
wrong[['A', 'B']\n['A', 'B']]: A generator can only be used once. The first `list` takes both values, so there's nothing left for the second.
wrong[('A', 'B')\n()]: Round brackets around a comprehension make a generator, not a tuple. `list(...)` turns its values into a list, with square brackets.
wrong[<generator object>\n[]]: `list(g)` asks the generator for all its values, so the first line shows them as a list.
hint: What does `list(...)` do with a generator?
hint: Can a generator hand out its values a second time?
right: The first `list(g)` asks for every value: 'A' and 'B'. After that the generator is used up, so the second `list(g)` gets nothing: `[]`.
wrong: `list(g)` collects the generator's values: `['A', 'B']`. A generator works only once, so the second call collects nothing and prints `[]`.

=== task: multi easy
title: Which give 6?
prompt: Choose **every** expression whose value is `6`.
* `sum([1, 2, 3])` :: Right. 1 + 2 + 3 is 6.
* `sum(n for n in range(4))` :: Right. `range(4)` is 0, 1, 2, 3, and 0 + 1 + 2 + 3 is 6.
* `sum(n * 2 for n in [1, 2])` :: Right. The doubled values are 2 and 4, which add up to 6.
* `max(n for n in [6, 2, 6])` :: Right. The biggest value is 6. It appears twice, but `max` gives it once.
- `len({1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3})` :: A set keeps each value once, so this set is `{1, 2, 3}` and its length is 3.
- `sum(1 for n in range(6) if n > 2)` :: This counts the numbers above 2 in 0 to 5: 3, 4 and 5. That's 3.
hint: Work out each one. For `sum(1 for ...)`, count how many items pass the filter.
hint: Four of them give 6.
right: The three sums all add up to 6, and the biggest of 6, 2, 6 is 6. The set has only 3 different items, and `sum(1 for ...)` counts the 3 numbers above 2.
wrong: Work them out: 6, 6, 6, 6, then 3 (the set `{1, 2, 3}`) and 3 (the count of 3, 4, 5).

=== task: parsons easy
title: How many colors?
prompt: Arrange the lines so that the program counts the different car colors, ignoring capital letters:

```output
3 colors
['blue', 'green', 'red']
```

Not every line is needed.
lines:
```python
cars = ["red", "Blue", "red", "blue", "green"]
colors = {c.lower() for c in cars}
print(len(colors), "colors")
print(sorted(colors))
```
distractors:
```python
colors = [c.lower() for c in cars]
colors = {c for c in cars}
```
hint: Lowercase each color, and collect them in a set so each appears once.
hint: A list would keep the repeats, and without `.lower()` "Blue" and "blue" stay different.
right: `{c.lower() for c in cars}` makes "Blue" and "blue" the same and keeps each color once: blue, green and red.
wrong: Order: the list, `colors = {c.lower() for c in cars}`, then the two prints. A list comprehension would count 5 colors, and a set without `.lower()` would count 4.

=== task: cells easy
title: Sum, max and count
prompt: After this code runs, what are the four variables?
code:
```python
nums = [5, 3, 8, 3, 5]
a = sum(nums)
b = max(n for n in nums)
c = len({n for n in nums})
d = sum(1 for n in nums if n > 4)
```
labels: a | b | c | d
answer: 24 | 8 | 3 | 3
wrong[24 | 8 | 5 | 3]: The set keeps each value once: 5, 3 and 8. So its length is 3.
wrong[24 | 8 | 3 | 18]: `sum(1 for ...)` adds 1 for each number that passes, so it counts them: 5, 8 and 5 are over 4, which is 3.
wrong[16 | 8 | 3 | 3]: `sum(nums)` adds every item, repeats included: 5 + 3 + 8 + 3 + 5 = 24.
hint: `sum` adds all the items. `max` gives the biggest.
hint: `c` is the number of **different** values. `d` adds 1 per number over 4.
right: The sum is 24 and the biggest is 8. The set holds 3 different values, and three numbers (5, 8, 5) are over 4, so the count is 3.
wrong: a = 5 + 3 + 8 + 3 + 5 = 24. b = 8. c = len({5, 3, 8}) = 3. d counts 5, 8 and 5: 3.

=== task: predict easy
title: Anyone over 18?
code:
```python
ages = [12, 17, 21]
print(any(a >= 18 for a in ages))
print(all(a >= 18 for a in ages))
```
wrong[False\nFalse]: `any` only needs **one** True value, and 21 >= 18 is True.
wrong[True\nTrue]: `all` needs **every** value to be True, but 12 and 17 are under 18.
wrong[[False, False, True]\nFalse]: `any` gives a single True or False, not a list.
hint: Is at least one age 18 or more?
hint: Is every age 18 or more?
right: 21 is 18 or more, so `any` is True. 12 and 17 aren't, so `all` is False.
wrong: `any` asks "is at least one True?": yes, 21. `all` asks "are all True?": no, 12 fails straight away.

=== task: mcq easy
title: A set of letters
prompt: What does this program print?
code:
```python
print({c for c in "hello"} == {"h", "e", "l", "o"})
```
check: output
* `True` :: Right. The set comprehension keeps one "l", so both sets hold h, e, l and o, and sets are equal when they hold the same items.
- `False` :: "hello" has two l's, but a set keeps each value once. Both sides hold the same four letters.
- `{'h', 'e', 'l', 'o'}` :: `==` compares the two sets and gives True or False. It doesn't print a set.
- `TypeError: unhashable type: 'set'` :: Comparing two sets with `==` is fine.
hint: How many l's does the set on the left keep?
hint: Does the order of items matter when comparing sets?
right: The comprehension gives h, e, l, o (one l). Sets compare by their items only, so the two sets are equal: True.
wrong: A set drops the second l, so both sets hold the same four letters, and `==` gives True.

=== task: code medium
title: How many different lengths?
prompt: Write a function `different_lengths(words)` that returns how many **different** word lengths there are in `words`. Use a set comprehension.

For example, `different_lengths(["tea", "jam", "toast"])` returns 2 (lengths 3 and 5).
starter:
```python
def different_lengths(words):
    # your code here
    return 0
```
solution:
```python
def different_lengths(words):
    return len({len(w) for w in words})
```
tests:
- different_lengths(["tea", "jam", "toast"])
- different_lengths([])
- different_lengths(["a", "bb", "ccc"])
- different_lengths(["hi", "hi", "ok"])
fail[1]: "tea" and "jam" both have 3 letters and "toast" has 5, so there are 2 different lengths. Collect the lengths in a set, then take its `len()`.
fail[2]: No words, no lengths: 0.
fail[3]: Three words, three different lengths: 3.
fail[4]: All three words have 2 letters, so there is only 1 different length.
error[NameError]: Use the parameter `words` inside the function.
forbid[.add(]: Use a set comprehension this time, not `.add()` in a loop.
hint: `{len(w) for w in words}` is the set of the different lengths.
hint: How many items does that set have?
hint: `return len({len(w) for w in words})`
right: The set comprehension collects each length once, so `len()` of the set is the number of different lengths. An empty list gives an empty set, of length 0.
wrong: Return `len({len(w) for w in words})`: the set of lengths, then how many items it has.

=== task: code medium
title: Count the long words
prompt: Write a function `count_long(words, n)` that returns how many words in `words` have **more than** `n` letters. Use `sum()` with a generator expression.

For example, `count_long(["sun", "planet", "moon"], 3)` returns 2.
starter:
```python
def count_long(words, n):
    # your code here
    return 0
```
solution:
```python
def count_long(words, n):
    return sum(1 for w in words if len(w) > n)
```
tests:
- count_long(["sun", "planet", "moon"], 3)
- count_long([], 2)
- count_long(["a", "bb"], 5)
- count_long(["ok", "ok"], 1)
- count_long(["tree", "tree", "a"], 3)
fail[1]: "planet" (6) and "moon" (4) have more than 3 letters, and "sun" has exactly 3, which isn't more. So the answer is 2. If you got 10, you added the lengths instead of 1 for each word.
fail[4]: Both words count, even though they're the same word: 2.
fail[5]: The two "tree"s each count once: 2.
error[NameError]: Use the parameters `words` and `n` inside the function.
require[sum(]: Count with `sum(1 for ...)`.
hint: Add 1 for every word that passes the test.
hint: The test is `len(w) > n`, at the end of the generator expression.
hint: `return sum(1 for w in words if len(w) > n)`
right: The generator hands `sum` a 1 for every word longer than `n`, so the total is the count. Repeated words count each time they appear, because nothing here removes duplicates.
wrong: Return `sum(1 for w in words if len(w) > n)`: one 1 per long word, added up.

=== task: predict medium
title: Lowercase and filter
code:
```python
words = ["Hi", "hi", "HI", "bye", "Bye"]
a = {w.lower() for w in words}
b = {w.lower() for w in words if w[0] == "B"}
print(sorted(a))
print(sorted(b))
print(len(words) - len(a))
```
wrong[['HI', 'Hi', 'bye', 'hi', 'Bye']\n['bye']\n0]: `.lower()` turns every word into small letters before it goes into the set, so the three his become one.
wrong[['bye', 'hi']\n[]\n3]: The filter checks the **original** word, before `.lower()`. "Bye" starts with a capital B, so it passes and becomes "bye".
wrong[['bye', 'hi']\n['bye']\n2]: 5 words went in and the set holds 2, so 3 were repeats.
hint: Lowercase every word first: hi, hi, hi, bye, bye.
hint: For `b`, the test `w[0] == "B"` looks at the word before it's lowercased.
right: Lowercased, the words are hi, hi, hi, bye, bye, so `a` holds 2 words. Only "Bye" starts with a capital B, and it goes in as "bye". 5 - 2 = 3 words were repeats.
wrong: `a` = {'hi', 'bye'}. `b` keeps only "Bye" (the test sees the original word) and adds it as "bye". `len(words) - len(a)` is 5 - 2 = 3.

