=== task: order medium
title: How any() stops
prompt: Put the steps of `any(n > 10 for n in [4, 15, 8])` in the order they happen.
items:
- `any` asks the generator for its first value
- The generator works out `4 > 10`, which is False
- `any` asks the generator for the next value
- The generator works out `15 > 10`, which is True
- `any` stops and gives True, without ever checking 8
hint: The generator only works out a value when `any` asks for one.
hint: `any` can stop as soon as it sees one True.
right: The generator is lazy: each value is worked out only when `any` asks for it. The first True ends the search, so 8 is never compared at all.
wrong: `any` asks, the generator answers False for 4, `any` asks again, the generator answers True for 15, and `any` stops there with True. 8 is never looked at.

=== task: multi medium
title: Which ones fail?
prompt: Choose **every** line that stops with an error.
* `max(n for n in [])` :: Right. There's nothing to compare: `ValueError: max() iterable argument is empty`.
- `sum(n for n in [])` :: With nothing to add, `sum` gives 0. No error.
- `any(n > 0 for n in [])` :: With nothing to check, `any` gives False.
- `all(n > 0 for n in [])` :: With nothing to check, nothing failed, so `all` gives True.
* `min([])` :: Right. An empty list has no smallest item: `ValueError: min() iterable argument is empty`.
* `sum(n for n in [1, 2], 10)` :: Right. With another argument in the call, the generator needs its own brackets: `SyntaxError: Generator expression must be parenthesized`.
hint: Which of these functions can give an answer for nothing at all?
hint: `sum`, `any` and `all` have an answer for nothing. Three lines fail.
right: `max` and `min` need at least one item. `sum` of nothing is 0, `any` is False and `all` is True. A generator next to another argument must be in its own brackets.
wrong: Empty input is fine for `sum` (0), `any` (False) and `all` (True), but not for `max` or `min`, which raise a ValueError. And `sum(n for n in [1, 2], 10)` needs brackets around the generator.

